gaslight
========

Flaring analysis for the Permian Basin. It cross-references satellite-detected
flares against Texas state records — permits, wells, leases, reported flaring
volumes, methane plumes — so you can see where gas is being burned and how that
squares with what operators tell regulators.

Live map: http://research.datadesk.eco/gaslight/

The data
--------

Everything lands in a single, standalone DuckDB file, `dist/gaslight.duckdb` —
one plain schema, lat/lon columns, no geometry blobs. Open it with the free
duckdb CLI (or read straight into pandas/R/QGIS):

    duckdb dist/gaslight.duckdb

Every table and column is documented in `docs/data-dictionary/`, and the same
notes live inside the database (`SELECT * FROM _dictionary`).

One thing worth knowing up front: the flaring *volumes* are self-reported by
operators to the RRC, while the satellite layers are independent observations
from space. They don't always agree — that gap is usually the point.

The Sentinel-2 flare layer is detected by the sibling permian-flaring project;
gaslight just fetches and displays it (`make s2`).

Building it
-----------

    uv sync
    make refresh     # rebuild the database from scratch
    make serve       # run the map locally on :8080
    make help        # everything else

Scrapers and the SQL pipeline live in `scripts/` and `queries/`; the web map is
in `web/` (MapLibre GL + DuckDB WASM, no build step, no npm). Push to main to
deploy via GitHub Pages.
